<!--
  patrol.xml: the patrol route (navigate_through_poses, 2026-11). Drive from
  the robot through every waypoint in order without stopping or turning at
  them, and finish at the last one like a MOVE: xy within the goal checker's
  0.10 m and heading within its 0.2 rad (FollowPath's default goal_checker).

  It is move.xml with two differences, and everything move.xml's header says
  -- the replan only on a path blocked for blocked_time or a changed goal,
  FollowPath's four refilling retries, the obstacle memory clears, the
  failure attribution by child order, the status sentences -- holds here
  unchanged and is not repeated. Tuning one of those numbers there (the
  retries against blocked_time above all) means tuning it here too.

  1. The planner branch plans through the waypoints:
     ComputePathThroughPoses (compute_path_through_poses) on
     {remaining_goals}, the waypoints still ahead. Octo3D plans it leg by
     leg, each later leg from the cell the one before ended on, so a
     waypoint upstairs is planned from upstairs; every pose of the path faces
     the next, and only the last waypoint's heading is kept, so the
     controllers turn only at the end (MPPI's final rotation and goal critics
     read the path's last pose). The navigator, not this tree, drops a
     waypoint from {remaining_goals} once the robot has passed within
     waypoint_passed_radius of it (blackboard_keys.hpp has why), so a replan
     mid-route starts from the next waypoint; {goals} keeps the client's list,
     which is what GlobalUpdatedGoal watches -- passing a waypoint is not a
     changed goal and does not replan.

  2. A route can pass back over itself (A -> B -> A, or a loop ending where it
     started). Nothing in the tree is specific to that; what makes it work is
     that every consumer of the path places the robot on it by forward-only
     progress (syncai_util/path_progress.hpp): MPPI's and RPP 3D's pruning,
     the controller server's goal gate (goal_reached_max_remaining_path) and
     feedback, and the planner server's `plan` trim and is_path_valid. And
     that the controller server cuts the path where it turns back on itself
     (syncai_util/path_cusps.hpp, cusp_min_angle): the robot stops on the
     turn-back waypoint and turns in place there -- the one place a patrol
     does stop short of its last waypoint -- because a controller handed the
     out-and-back whole stopped short of it for good (robot01, 2026-11-02).

  The failure attribution maps ComputePathThroughPoses to PLAN_FAILED, but
  navigate_through_poses is nav2's action with an Empty result: the reason is
  in the task_runner log only, until its syncai_common twin exists.
-->
<root main_tree_to_execute="MainTree">
  <BehaviorTree ID="MainTree">
    <Sequence name="NavigateFromFreshGlobalCostmap">
      <ForceSuccess>
        <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearDynamicOverlay-NewGoal" service_name="planner_server/Octo3D/clear_overlay" server_timeout="1000"/>
      </ForceSuccess>
      <PipelineSequence name="NavigateWithReplanning">
        <RateController hz="1.0">
          <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="ComputePathThroughPoses">
            <Fallback name="FallbackComputePathThroughPoses">
              <ReactiveSequence name="CheckIfNewPathNeeded">
                <Inverter>
                  <GlobalUpdatedGoal/>
                </Inverter>
                <IsPathValid path="{path}" server_timeout="100" blocked_time="10.0"/>
              </ReactiveSequence>
              <ComputePathThroughPoses goals="{remaining_goals}" path="{path}" planner_id="Octo3D"/>
            </Fallback>
            <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearDynamicOverlay-Context" service_name="planner_server/Octo3D/clear_overlay"/>
          </RecoveryNode>
        </RateController>
        <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="4" retry_refill_time="60.0" name="FollowPath">
          <FollowPath path="{path}" controller_id="FollowPath"/>
          <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-Context" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
        </RecoveryNode>
      </PipelineSequence>
    </Sequence>
  </BehaviorTree>
</root>
